Based on the provided time-series plot for the "complex_tank" system, here is an
analysis of the behavior of $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$:

### General Behavior
All three variables exhibit a **sawtooth pattern**, characterized by slow, 
linear decreases (discharging/draining) and rapid, linear increases 
(charging/refilling). 

*   **$x_1$ (Dark Blue):** Starts at its highest value ($\approx 24.2$). It 
drains steadily for a long period until $t \approx 6.0$ s, where it reaches a 
lower threshold of $\approx 15.0$. It then refills rapidly until $t \approx 6.5$
s (reaching a value of $20.0$), before draining again.
*   **$x_2$ (Brown):** Starts at $\approx 20.3$. It drains until $t \approx 4.3$
s, refills to $20.0$ by $t \approx 4.9$ s, drains again until $t \approx 9.0$ s,
and refills once more.
*   **$x_3$ (Light Blue):** Starts at its lowest value ($\approx 14.7$). It 
immediately begins refilling, reaching $20.0$ by $t \approx 0.6$ s. It drains 
until $t \approx 6.5$ s and then refills until $t \approx 7.1$ s.

### Can multiple variables charge simultaneously?
**No, multiple variables cannot charge simultaneously.**

The system appears to be governed by a mutual exclusion rule for refilling. By 
observing the slopes of the lines:
*   When any variable has a positive slope (charging), the other two always have
negative slopes (discharging).
*   For example, at $t \approx 6.5$ s, $x_1$ completes its charging phase 
exactly as $x_3$ begins its charging phase. They "hand off" the charging state.
*   At no point in the 10-second window are two variables increasing at the same
time.

This suggests a hybrid system where a single shared resource (like a pump or 
refill inlet) can only serve one tank at a time. The system switches between 
discrete modes: "All Draining," "Refilling $x_1$," "Refilling $x_2$," and 
"Refilling $x_3$."
